Infrastructure Monitoring & Automation

Monitoring Overview

For Task 3, I implemented basic infrastructure and application monitoring for the deployed React and Express application using the monitoring capabilities provided by Render.

The backend application is deployed as a Render Web Service. A dedicated `/health` endpoint was added to the Express application to provide a simple way of checking whether the application is running and responding correctly.

The health endpoint returns an HTTP `200` response with information about the service status and a timestamp:

json
{
  "status": "healthy",
  "service": "buildlabs-backend",
  "timestamp": "current-time"
}

Render was configured to use:
text
/health

as the application's health-check path.

Areas Monitored

The monitoring implementation covers the following areas:

| Monitoring Area          | Implementation                                  |
| ------------------------ | ----------------------------------------------- |
| Application availability | Render deployment status and `/health` endpoint |
| Health monitoring        | Render Health Check using `/health`             |
| Deployment status        | Render deployment dashboard                     |
| Application logs         | Render Logs                                     |
| Error logs               | Render runtime/error logs                       |
| Network activity         | Render outbound bandwidth metrics               |
| Memory resources         | 512 MB resource limit displayed by Render       |
| CPU resources            | 0.15 CPU resource limit displayed by Render     |
| Deployment performance   | Render deployment duration/status               |

The monitoring screenshots demonstrate successful deployments, application startup, health-check configuration, logs, errors, bandwidth activity, and the available infrastructure resource information.

Detailed CPU and memory utilization graphs were not available under the selected Render free compute plan. Therefore, monitoring focused on the metrics and observability features supported by the current environment, including service health, logs, deployment events, resource limits, and network activity.

 Automation Implemented

Two important automation improvements were incorporated into the infrastructure.

Automated deployment: Changes pushed to the GitHub repository trigger the CI workflow. The application is validated and built through GitHub Actions, while Render provides automated cloud deployment from the connected repository.

Automated health checking:** Render periodically checks the `/health` endpoint to determine whether the backend service is healthy and available.

These improvements reduce manual deployment work and provide faster detection of application problems.

