4. Infrastructure Documentation

 Project Overview

The BuildLabs DevOps Internship project is a full-stack web application consisting of a React/Vite frontend and an Express/Node.js backend.

The project was used to demonstrate practical DevOps concepts including source control, containerization, CI/CD, cloud deployment, environment configuration, monitoring, automation, and incident response.

 Technology Stack

| Component                  | Technology                           |
| -------------------------- | ------------------------------------ |
| Source Control             | Git & GitHub                         |
| Frontend                   | React + Vite                         |
| Backend                    | Node.js + Express                    |
| Containerization           | Docker                               |
| Multi-container management | Docker Compose                       |
| CI                         | GitHub Actions                       |
| Cloud Platform             | Render                               |
| Monitoring                 | Render Logs, Metrics & Health Checks |
| API communication          | HTTPS/REST                           |
| Configuration              | Environment variables                |

 Source Control

The application source code is maintained in GitHub. Git is used locally for version control, while GitHub provides remote repository hosting and integration with the CI/CD pipeline.

Development changes follow the general workflow:

bash
git add .
git commit -m "descriptive commit message"
git push origin main

Docker Infrastructure

The frontend and backend are containerized separately.

The frontend Docker image uses a Node.js build stage to compile the React/Vite application and Nginx to serve the generated frontend.

The backend Docker image runs the Node.js/Express application.

Docker Compose provides local orchestration of the services.

The local architecture uses:

text
Frontend:
Host 8080 → Container 80

Backend:
Host 4000 → Container 4000

CI Pipeline

GitHub Actions provides Continuous Integration.

The `Test and Build Application` workflow automatically validates application changes.

The workflow performs tasks including:

text
Checkout source code
        ↓
Configure Node.js
        ↓
Install backend dependencies
        ↓
Backend syntax/quality check
        ↓
Install frontend dependencies
        ↓
Build frontend
        ↓
Validate Docker Compose
        ↓
Build Docker images
        ↓
Pipeline Success

A successful GitHub Actions execution was recorded, demonstrating that the application could pass the configured checks and builds before deployment.

Cloud Infrastructure

Render is used as the cloud deployment platform.

The backend is deployed as a Web Service and runs the Express application in the cloud.

The application supports Render's dynamically assigned port using:

javascript
const PORT = process.env.PORT || 4000;

and binds to:

text
0.0.0.0

This enables the service to operate correctly inside the cloud/container environment.

 Environment Variables

Environment-specific values are kept outside the application source code where appropriate.

The frontend uses:

env
VITE_API_URL=<backend-url>

to identify the deployed backend.

The backend uses:

text
process.env.PORT

to receive the port provided by the hosting platform.

This prevents environment-specific URLs and ports from being unnecessarily hard-coded.

Monitoring Infrastructure

Monitoring is provided through several mechanisms:

Health checks: `/health` provides a machine-readable indication of backend availability.

Logs: Render application and deployment logs provide information about startup, runtime activity, errors, and deployment failures.

Metrics: Render's metrics dashboard provides available infrastructure and bandwidth information.

Deployment events: Render records deployment status and history.

Together, these provide basic observability into the deployed infrastructure.

5. Final Deployment Documentation

 Deployment Process

The final deployment process begins when application changes are committed and pushed to GitHub.

 Step 1 – Source Code

Changes are committed:

bash
git add .
git commit -m "update application"
git push origin main

Step 2 – Continuous Integration

The push triggers GitHub Actions.

The CI pipeline validates the backend, builds the frontend, validates the Docker Compose configuration, and builds the application's Docker images.

If a step fails, the workflow reports the failure for investigation.

Step 3 – Cloud Deployment

The GitHub repository is connected to Render.

Render obtains the application source and performs the configured deployment process.

The backend starts using Node.js/Express and listens on the port supplied by Render.

Successful logs include messages indicating that the server is listening on `0.0.0.0` and that the service is live.

Step 4 – Environment Configuration

The frontend receives the backend base URL through:

env
VITE_API_URL=<backend-url>

The backend receives its production port through:

javascript
process.env.PORT

Step 5 – Health Verification

After deployment, the backend is verified through:

text
GET /health

A successful response indicates:

json
{
  "status": "healthy",
  "service": "buildlabs-backend",
  "timestamp": "..."
}

 Step 6 – Monitoring

After deployment, Render is used to observe:

text
Deployment status
Application availability
Health-check status
Application logs
Error logs
Network/bandwidth activity
Available infrastructure resource information

This allows deployment and runtime problems to be investigated from a central platform.


Task 3 Reflection

During this project, I learned that DevOps goes beyond simply deploying an application. It involves combining development, operations, automation, monitoring, documentation, and problem-solving to create a reliable software delivery process.

Working with Git, Docker, GitHub Actions, and Render helped me understand how different DevOps tools work together. I learned how to containerize frontend and backend services, build a CI pipeline, deploy applications to a cloud platform, manage environment variables, implement application health checks, monitor logs and infrastructure information, and troubleshoot deployment problems.

Automation improves software delivery by reducing repetitive manual work and making processes more consistent. Automated CI checks help identify problems before deployment, while automatic cloud deployment and health checks make application delivery and monitoring more reliable.

One of the most valuable parts of the project was troubleshooting real problems, including port conflicts, incorrect working directories, missing `package.json` errors, cloud deployment configuration, frontend/backend communication, and health-check issues. These challenges improved my ability to investigate problems using logs and command-line tools instead of only following predefined deployment steps.

With more time, I would improve the project by introducing more advanced observability with tools such as Prometheus and Grafana, automated alerts, persistent application data, automated backups, stronger automated testing, security scanning, Infrastructure as Code, and a more advanced deployment strategy such as staging and production environments.

Overall, this project gave me practical experience with the complete DevOps lifecycle: **build, containerize, automate, deploy, monitor, troubleshoot, and document.**

